 ## Overview
-From-region visibility preprocessing is a technique that precomputes which parts of a 3D scene are visible from specific regions of the camera space, allowing for aggressive culling of occluded geometry during rendering [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard face culling by operating on large geometric clusters or regions rather than individual voxel faces, and it often incorporates occluder fusion to reduce the complexity of the visibility test [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [Face Culling](8735-face-culling.md) which relies on local adjacency, this method solves the problem of global occlusion for large datasets, albeit at the cost of significant preprocessing time [S1, unofficial].
+From-region visibility preprocessing is a technique that precomputes the visibility of geometric regions (such as clusters of triangles or voxels) from specific viewpoints or regions of interest to optimize rendering performance [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of expensive per-frame visibility determination by shifting the computational burden to an offline or semi-offline phase [S1, unofficial]. Unlike parent methods that rely on local voxel adjacency for face culling, this approach handles complex, non-axis-aligned geometry and occluder fusion, distinguishing it from simpler block-based culling [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method involves offline analysis of the scene geometry to build a visibility map or occluder hierarchy.
+The method involves generating a visibility map or set of visibility primitives that indicate which parts of the scene are visible from given viewpoints. The core inputs are the scene geometry (often represented as a collection of triangles or cells) and a set of viewpoints or viewing regions [S1, unofficial]. The output is a data structure mapping viewpoints to visible subsets of the scene geometry [S1, unofficial].
 
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-- Input: A 3D scene model (typically represented as a triangle mesh or voxelized environment).
-- Output: A precomputed visibility structure (e.g., occluder lists, visibility polygons) associated with specific camera regions.
